{"records":[{"id":"v6cszczy48","title":"Kinase Inhibitors Screen Tamoxifen Resistant Breast Cancer","description":"Antiestrogen resistance of breast cancer has been related to enhanced growth factor receptor expression and activation. We have previously shown that ectopic expression and subsequent activation of the insulin-like growth factor-1 receptor (IGF1R) or the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR) in MCF7 or T47D breast cancer cells results in antiestrogen resistance. In order to identify critical therapeutic targets that mediate this antiestrogen resistance, we performed a kinase inhibitor screen with 273 different inhibitors. Various ALK inhibitors, including TEA684, AZD3463 and LDK378, inhibited cell proliferation in IGF1R expressing cells under both normal and antiestrogen resistance conditions by preventing IGF1R activation and subsequent downstream signaling; ALK inhibitors did not affect EGFR signaling. On the other hand, MEK1/2 inhibitors, including PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733, selectively antagonized IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance but did not affect cell proliferation under normal growth conditions. The MEK inhibitors PD0325901, selumetinib, trametinib and TAK733 caused an arrest in the G0/G1 cell cycle phase only under antiestrogen resistance conditions in IGF1R expressing cells, but not under normal growth conditions. RNAseq analysis revealed that MEK inhibitors PD0325901 and selumetinib drastically altered cell cycle progression and cell migration networks under IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance. In a group of 291 patients with primary operable ER+ breast cancer, strong pMEK staining in metastatic breast cancer after first-line tamoxifen treatment was significantly related to no clinical benefit. We propose a critical role for MEK activation in IGF1R signaling-mediated antiestrogen resistance and anticipate that dual-targeted therapy with MEK inhibitor and antiestrogen could improve treatment outcome.  ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-06T16:25:15.469Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/v6cszczy48","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/v6cszczy48"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"John","last_name":"Meerman","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Breast Cancer","Estrogen","Tyrosine Kinase Inhibitor","Tamoxifen"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hw4286gycz","title":"Small molecular inhibitors of NRF2 (physicochemical-, spectral-, pharmacological properties) and their binding to KEAP1 (docking studies)","description":"Official supporting information document associated with the manuscript entitled \"Pharmacological targeting of nuclear factor (erythroid-derived 2)-like 2 (NRF2) to improve the efficacy of oncological photodynamic therapy\" that was submitted to Biochemical Pharmacology. The supporting document contains information on small-molecular inhibitors of NRF2 (physicochemical properties, spectral data, toxicity, posology). Furthermore, the supporting documents contains methodological details related to the computational chemical analyses (molecular dynamics and simulations) performed on the inhibitors and their interaction with KEAP1, a cellular redox sensor.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-15T21:32:00.013Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hw4286gycz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hw4286gycz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"de Klerk","orcid":"0000-0001-6245-8226"},{"first_name":"Mark","last_name":"de Keijzer","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Leonardo","last_name":"Franchi","orcid":"0000-0002-9551-472X"},{"first_name":"Jiahao","last_name":"Tian","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maxim","last_name":"Gureev","orcid":"0000-0002-0385-922X"},{"first_name":"Yingnan","last_name":"Ge","orcid":"0000-0001-9151-5031"},{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mesquita","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gideon","last_name":"Saelman","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Isis","last_name":"Van herteryck","orcid":"0000-0001-8607-0445"},{"first_name":"Lionel","last_name":"Mendes Dias","orcid":"0000-0002-5305-6812"},{"first_name":"Lianne","last_name":"de Haan","orcid":"0000-0002-8124-2815"},{"first_name":"Mingjuan","last_name":"Li","orcid":"0000-0001-9214-9336"},{"first_name":"Lenka","last_name":"Boyd","orcid":"0000-0002-7441-6696"},{"first_name":"Tessa","last_name":"Le Large","orcid":"0000-0002-4273-7217"},{"first_name":"Elisa","last_name":"Giovannetti","orcid":"0000-0002-7565-7504"},{"first_name":"Geert","last_name":"Kazemier","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Perry","last_name":"Moerland","orcid":"0000-0002-2357-3659"},{"first_name":"Yunlei","last_name":"Li","orcid":"0000-0002-5697-1602"},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Stubbs","orcid":"0000-0001-9817-9982"},{"first_name":"Mathias","last_name":"Devreese","orcid":"0000-0003-2512-4176"},{"first_name":"Yuri","last_name":"Porozov","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joanne","last_name":"Verheij","orcid":"0000-0003-1283-630X"},{"first_name":"Twan","last_name":"Lammers","orcid":"0000-0002-1090-6805"},{"first_name":"Yazan","last_name":"Khaled","orcid":"0000-0001-6571-3579"},{"first_name":"Byron","last_name":"Baron","orcid":"0000-0001-5722-6295"},{"first_name":"Lingfeng","last_name":"Wu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michal","last_name":"Heger","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"Zhejiang Provincial Key Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"Z20H160031"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0368jnd28","name":"Dutch Cancer Society","grant_id":"10666"},{"id":null,"name":"Fundacao de Amparo a Pesquisa do Estado de Sao Paolo","grant_id":"CNPJ 43.828.151/0001-45"},{"id":null,"name":"Zhejiang Provincial Foreign Expert Program Grant","grant_id":"201812"},{"id":null,"name":"Chinese Scholarship Council","grant_id":"202108330069"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00c489v88","name":"Academy of Medical Sciences","grant_id":"SGL027/1011"},{"id":null,"name":"Science and Technology Bureau of Jiaxing","grant_id":"2019AY32012"},{"id":null,"name":"Jiaxing Government - Jiaxing Key Laboratory for Photonanomedicine and Experimental Therapeutics","grant_id":"2019"},{"id":null,"name":"Chinese Scholarship Council","grant_id":"202308320054"},{"id":null,"name":"Fundação para a Ciência e a Tecnologia ","grant_id":"SFRH/BD/140283/2018"},{"id":null,"name":"Zhejiang Provincial Basic Public Welfare Research Project ","grant_id":"LGD21H300001"}],"categories":["Toxicology","Pharmacodynamics","Photodynamic Therapy","Molecular Docking","Molecular Biopharmaceutics","Adjuvant Chemotherapy","Transcription Factor Nrf2","NRF2/KEAP1 Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"b232zdtg3m","title":"Isoorientin data","description":"Research Data of the  manuscript \"Isoorientin reverses lung cancer drug resistance by promoting ferroptosis via the SIRT6/Nrf2/GPX4 signaling pathway\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-13T14:57:00.662Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/b232zdtg3m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/b232zdtg3m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"senling","last_name":"feng","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Reference Material","Database"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"kggn4n3ynb","title":"Bone loss is not prevented by calcium and Vitamin D supplementation in postmenopausal breast cancer patients receiving aromatase inhibitors","description":"We retrospectively analyzed 636 breast cancer patients underwent surgery at Ewha Womans University Mokdong Hospital (Seoul, Republic of Korea) between Jan. 2012 and Jun. 2018. All patients were postmenopausal, hormone receptor positive and received aromatase inhibitors as adjuvant therapy for 5 years. Patients receiving aromatase inhibitors as switching or extended therapy were excluded because prior SERM (Tamoxifen) could affect BMD change in the early period of aromatase inhibitors administration.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-07-20T19:09:45.354Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/kggn4n3ynb","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/kggn4n3ynb"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"YOONHEE","last_name":"SHIN","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Therapy for Breast Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4rk3nntzbs","title":"Bromodomain and HDAC inhibitors equivalently overcome PI3K/AKT inhibition resistance in cancers","description":"Blocking BRD4 or HDACs induces paradoxically similar gene expression, comparably attenuates PI3K/AKT inhibitor resistance in multiple cancer cells. PI3K/AKT inhibitors induce global loss of histone acetylation. While in the resistant cells, specific genome loci gain H3K27 acetylation and BRD4 binding coupled with up-regulation of selective genes. These loci and genes are vulnerable to BRD4 or CBP/p300 bromodomain inhibitors, as well as HDAC inhibitors. Inadequate occupancy of HDACs on these loci permits gain of H3K27 acetylation and BRD4 recruitment after AKT inhibition. Bromodomain inhibitors directly suppress these loci; HDAC inhibitors induce histone hyper-acetylation outside these loci, offering competitive BRD4 binding sites, re-localizing BRD4. To suppress these loci, genes, and relative resistance, multi-bromodomain inhibitor NEO2734 and HDAC inhibitors are equivalent in effects. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-09-27T20:42:13.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4rk3nntzbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4rk3nntzbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"DI","last_name":"WU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Histone Deacetylase","Gene Transcription","Histone Code"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f5jtt3s8hv","title":"GCN2 is a determinant of the response to WEE1 kinase inhibition in small-cell lung cancer ","description":"Patients with small-cell lung cancer (SCLC) are in dire need of more effective therapeutic options. Frequent disruption of the G1 checkpoint in SCLC cells creates a dependency on the G2/M checkpoint to maintain genomic integrity. Indeed, in pre-clinical models, inhibiting the G2/M checkpoint kinase WEE1 shows promise in inhibiting SCLC growth. However, toxicity and acquired resistance have limited the clinical effectiveness of this strategy. Here, using CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens in vitro and in vivo, we identified multiple factors influencing the response of SCLC cells to the WEE1 kinase inhibitor AZD1775, including the GCN2 kinase and other members of its signaling pathway. Rapid activation of GCN2 upon AZD1775 treatment triggers a stress response in SCLC cells. Pharmacological or genetic activation of the GCN2 pathway enhances cancer cell killing by AZD1775. Thus, activation of the GCN2 pathway represents a novel approach for augmenting the efficacy of WEE1 inhibitors in SCLC.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-26T09:21:15.032Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f5jtt3s8hv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f5jtt3s8hv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexander","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":"0000-0001-9081-9850"},{"first_name":"Julien","last_name":"Sage","orcid":"0000-0002-8928-9968"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Molecular Biology","Flow Cytometry","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9cwkxznpp2","title":"Sánchez-Ortega el al A potential therapeutic strategy based on acute oxidative stress induction  for wild-type NRF2/KEAP1 lung squamous cell carcinoma","description":"We hypothesized that the frequency of NRF2 activation in LUSC (35%) may reflect a sensitivity of LUSC to ROS. Results from this study reveal that whereas tumors containing active forms of NRF2 were protected, ROS induction in wild-type NFE2L2/KEAP1 LUSC cells triggered ferroptosis. The mechanism of ROS action in normal-NRF2 LUSC cells involved transient NRF2 activation, miR-126-3p/miR-126-5p upregulation, and reduction of p85β and SETD5 levels. SETD5 levels reduction triggered pentose pathway gene levels increase to toxic values. Simultaneous depletion of p85βPI3K and SETD5 triggered LUSC cell death, while p85βPI3K and SETD5 overexpression rescued survival of ROS-treated normal-NRF2 LUSC cells. This shows that the cascade involving NRF2 > miR-126-3p, miR-126-5p > p85βPI3K and SETD5 is responsible for ROS-induced cell death in normal-NRF2 LUSC","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-22T09:23:16.463Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9cwkxznpp2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9cwkxznpp2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Miriam","last_name":"Sanchez-Ortega","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ana","last_name":"Carrera","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Datapath"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"7dpx2854m8","title":"An acetyl-histone vulnerability in PI3K/AKT inhibition resistant cancers is targetable by both BET and HDAC inhibitors","description":"Acquisition of resistance to PI3K/AKT-targeted monotherapy implies the existence of common resistance mechanisms independent of cancer type. Here we demonstrate that PI3K/AKT inhibitors cause glycolytic crisis, acetyl-CoA shortage and a global decrease in histone acetylation. Also, PI3K/AKT inhibitors induce drug resistance by selectively augmenting H3K27 acetylation and binding of CBP/p300 and BRD4 proteins at a subset of growth factor and receptor (GF/R) gene loci. BRD4 occupation at these loci and drug resistant cell growth are vulnerable to both bromodomain and HDAC inhibitors. Little or none occupation of HDAC proteins at the GF/R gene loci underscores the paradox that cells respond equivalently to the two classes of inhibitors with opposite modes of action. Targeting this unique acetyl-histone related vulnerability offers two clinically viable strategies to overcome PI3K/AKT inhibitor resistance in different cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-02-04T13:26:30.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7dpx2854m8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7dpx2854m8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Haojie","last_name":"Huang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"m6vrdbwsnc","title":"Raw, uncropped supporting Western blot files for: Lignitto L. et al. ... Pagano M.,  Nrf2 activation promotes lung cancer metastasis by inhibiting the degradation of Bach1. Cell 2019","description":"Raw, not cropped supporting Western blot files for: Lignitto L., LeBoeuf S., Homer H., Jiang S., Askenazi M., Karakousi T., Pass H., Bhutkar A., Tsirigos A., Ueberheide B., Sayin V., Papagiannakopoulos T, and Pagano M.  Nrf2 activation promotes lung cancer metastasis by inhibiting the degradation of Bach1.  Cell 2019.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-06-27T18:52:48.001Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/m6vrdbwsnc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/m6vrdbwsnc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Pagano","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luca","last_name":"Lignitto","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology","Cancer","Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"yhs2hskx9r","title":"CACNA1S haploinsufficiency confers resistance to New World arenavirus infection ","description":"Primary and raw data from the manuscript \"CACNA1S haploinsufficiency confers resistance to New World arenavirus infection\" ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-